$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: safekeys/deployment-packaging
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-26"
summary: |
  Reproducible, hardened packaging so Safekeys can run on a real host: container images for the
  control plane and sidecar, a production Compose file, and systemd units that carry the OS
  isolation the architecture promises. Production refuses every development shortcut — dev-mode
  OpenBao, a hardcoded root token, an ad-hoc signing seed, and the insecure local keystore.
$system: safekeys/system
$service: safekeys/system
status: built
intent: |
  The software MVP works end to end, but "works on the author's laptop" and "safe to run on a VPS"
  are different claims. Deployment packaging closes the gap without touching the agent-facing
  protocol: the token and folder formats do not change, only how the authority and the sidecar are
  built, confined, and started. It is also where the architecture's process-isolation promise stops
  being prose and becomes enforceable configuration — the sidecar's separate OS user, seccomp-style
  system-call filtering, and AppArmor confinement land here, not in application code.

  The guiding rule is deny-by-default applied to operations: a production unit must be unable to
  select a development key path even by accident, and a hardening regression must fail a test
  rather than pass silently.
flows:
  - id: build-images
    name: Build the service images
    description: Multi-stage Dockerfiles produce minimal, non-root images for the control plane and the sidecar.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: >-
          A builder stage compiles a static, CGO-free Go binary for each service; a runtime stage
          copies only the binary and CA certificates.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: >-
          Each image declares a fixed non-root UID/GID, a read-only-friendly layout, and no shell
          or package manager in the runtime layer.
      - id: s3
        action: validate
        target: The build asserts that no image layer contains a signing key, KEK, API key, or secret.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: Local images are tagged for Compose; CI publishes them for a real host.
  - id: publish-images
    name: Publish images by digest
    description: CI builds and pushes the images so a host runs exactly what was tested.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: send
        target: CI builds both images from the committed Dockerfiles.
      - id: s2
        action: record
        target: Images are pushed to a registry and referenced by immutable digest, never a moving tag.
      - id: s3
        action: validate
        target: The deploy manifest pins the digest, so a pull cannot silently change the artefact.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: A previous digest can be re-run for rollback without rebuilding.
  - id: deploy-single-vm
    name: Deploy to a single hardened VM
    description: A reference install on one host — users, directories, secrets, and start order.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: >-
          The install creates a dedicated sidecar OS user distinct from any agent user, plus a
          shared group whose sole purpose is granting access to the sidecar socket.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: >-
          Secrets (control-plane signing seed, API key, OpenBao token) are placed as root-owned
          0600 material and delivered to services as systemd credentials or Compose secrets.
      - id: s3
        action: setup
        target: >-
          systemd units start the key store, then the control plane, then the sidecar, with
          dependency ordering and restart policy expressed in the units themselves.
      - id: s4
        action: validate
        target: The units carry the confinement directives and pass the asserted hardening threshold.
      - id: s5
        action: observe
        target: >-
          An agent process running as its own user, in the shared group, resolves a token through
          the socket; no other local user can connect.
  - id: provision-prod-keystore
    name: Provision a production key store
    description: OpenBao runs as a persistent, sealed-at-rest server — not the in-memory dev server.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: >-
          OpenBao is configured for persistent storage with TLS, and a real init produces sealed
          unseal shares rather than a fixed root token.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: The transit engine is enabled and the KEK is created; the KEK never leaves the store.
      - id: s3
        action: authenticate
        target: >-
          The control plane and sidecar receive narrowly scoped tokens limited to the transit
          operations they need, not the root token.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: A missing or sealed key store denies resolution rather than falling back to a local KEK.
  - id: provision-signing-key
    name: Provision the token signing key
    description: The Ed25519 signing seed is a root-only credential until HSM custody lands in Phase 1.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: >-
          An operator generates an Ed25519 seed and installs it as a root-owned 0600 credential on
          the control-plane host, never in a shell profile, image layer, or Compose environment block.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: The unit loads the seed as a systemd credential or Compose secret and exposes it only to the control-plane process.
      - id: s3
        action: validate
        target: The sidecar never receives the seed; it holds public keys only.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: >-
          HSM or secure-element custody remains the Phase 1 target; this flow is the explicit,
          auditable holding pattern, not a replacement for it.
contracts:
  - id: no-dev-credentials-in-prod
    description: Production artifacts must be unable to select a development credential path.
    must_have:
      - Production uses a persistent OpenBao server, never `server -dev`, and never a fixed root token
      - The signing seed is delivered from root-owned secret material, not a shell export or the dev key file
      - No production artifact sets SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE or SAFEKEYS_REVOCATION_OFFLINE
      - The development Compose file is not a supported production artefact
  - id: least-privilege-containers
    description: Each image runs with the minimum privilege it needs and nothing more.
    must_have:
      - Both services run as a fixed non-root UID/GID
      - The runtime image contains no shell, package manager, or build toolchain
      - No image layer contains a signing key, KEK, API key, or plaintext secret
      - Containers drop all Linux capabilities except those a service genuinely requires
      - The sidecar's root filesystem is read-only apart from its socket and folder directories
  - id: sidecar-host-isolation
    description: The OS-level confinement the architecture promises is enforced by the unit and asserted by a test.
    must_have:
      - The sidecar runs as an OS user distinct from any agent user
      - The unit sets NoNewPrivileges, a system-call filter, and the Protect* / Restrict* families
      - The sidecar shares no memory with agent processes (private tmp, no shared dev/shm, IPC removed)
      - An AppArmor profile or SELinux type confines the binary on hosts that support one
      - A hardening regression fails `systemd-analyze security` against a pinned exposure threshold
  - id: socket-access-model
    description: The socket is reachable by agents running as a distinct user, and by no one else.
    must_have:
      - The socket is owned by the sidecar user and a dedicated shared group
      - Socket mode grants the group access and denies all others; a world-accessible socket is a failure
      - A process not in the group cannot connect, even as a local user
      - The access model is consistent with the separate-user decision rather than contradicting it
  - id: fail-closed-on-boot
    description: A production start that cannot verify authority must not serve.
    must_have:
      - The sidecar refuses to start when it cannot load public keys or resolve-time policy
      - The sidecar refuses to start when the configured key store is unreachable or sealed
      - Services declare dependencies and restart policy so ordering is enforced, not assumed
      - Deny-by-default policy is retained; production exposes no offline or cached-allow fallback
  - id: reproducible-deploy
    description: A host runs exactly the artefact that was tested.
    must_have:
      - Images are referenced by immutable digest, never by a moving tag such as latest
      - The build is reproducible from the committed Dockerfiles and pinned base images
      - A rollback to a prior digest requires no rebuild
      - The deploy inputs (units, Compose file, config) are version-controlled
tests:
  - id: images-run-nonroot
    flow: build-images
    setup:
      image: sidecar-and-control-plane
    expect:
      - assertion: each container's process runs as a non-root UID
      - assertion: the runtime image contains no shell or package manager
    contracts:
      - least-privilege-containers
  - id: images-contain-no-secrets
    flow: build-images
    setup:
      scan: image-layers
    expect:
      - assertion: no layer contains a signing seed, KEK, API key, or plaintext secret
    contracts:
      - least-privilege-containers
      - no-dev-credentials-in-prod
  - id: prod-refuses-dev-credentials
    flow: provision-prod-keystore
    setup:
      profile: production
    expect:
      - assertion: startup fails when only the insecure local keystore is configured
      - assertion: startup fails when a fixed development key store token is supplied
      - assertion: no production unit references SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE
    contracts:
      - no-dev-credentials-in-prod
  - id: sidecar-hardening-asserted
    flow: deploy-single-vm
    setup:
      command: systemd-analyze security safekeys-sidecar
    expect:
      - assertion: the reported exposure level is at or below the pinned threshold
      - assertion: NoNewPrivileges and the system-call filter are active
    contracts:
      - sidecar-host-isolation
  - id: socket-not-world-accessible
    flow: deploy-single-vm
    setup:
      agent_user: safekeys-agent
      other_user: someone-else
    expect:
      - assertion: the agent user in the sidecar group can connect and resolve
      - assertion: a user outside the group cannot connect
      - assertion: the socket mode denies access to unaffiliated local users
    contracts:
      - socket-access-model
  - id: boot-fails-closed
    flow: deploy-single-vm
    setup:
      keystore: unreachable
    expect:
      - assertion: the sidecar exits rather than serving a degraded resolution
      - assertion: systemd restart policy does not mask the failure from monitoring
    contracts:
      - fail-closed-on-boot
  - id: deploy-pins-digest
    flow: publish-images
    setup:
      manifest: deploy/compose.prod.yml
    expect:
      - assertion: every image reference is pinned by digest
      - assertion: no service resolves a moving tag
    contracts:
      - reproducible-deploy
decisions:
  - id: systemd-single-vm-first
    decision: >-
      Ship systemd units plus a production Compose file targeting a single hardened VM as the
      reference deployment; defer Kubernetes manifests.
    rationale: >-
      The system map's own footprint is "2–3 small VMs or one small Kubernetes namespace", and the
      sidecar's trust boundary is the host, which systemd expresses directly. Adding a cluster
      orchestrator early would multiply configuration surface without adding a security property,
      and the agent-facing interface is unaffected either way, so Kubernetes can land later behind
      the same images and units.
    alternatives:
      - option: Kubernetes manifests now
        rejected_because: >-
          No cluster exists to justify them, and network policies and pod security contexts would
          duplicate the host-level confinement that is the actual isolation mechanism.
    status: accepted
  - id: os-isolation-via-systemd
    decision: >-
      Fold the sidecar's OS isolation into the systemd unit's hardening directives, with an AppArmor
      profile where the host supports it, rather than deferring it as separate work.
    rationale: >-
      The system map already specifies seccomp/AppArmor confinement and a separate user; the unit is
      exactly where those are expressed and where they can be asserted by a test. Leaving it out
      would ship a deployment whose stated guarantee the artifact does not enforce.
    status: accepted
  - id: socket-shared-group
    decision: >-
      Change the sidecar socket from 0600 to group-access (sidecar user plus a dedicated shared
      group, group-only mode) so agent processes running as a distinct user can connect.
    rationale: >-
      The separate-os-user decision requires agents to be a different user from the sidecar, but a
      0600 socket is reachable only by the sidecar's own user — the two are contradictory, and today
      it works only because development runs everything as one account. A narrow shared group
      restores the intended least privilege: agents may connect, nothing else local may. This
      refines the separate-user decision rather than reversing it.
    status: accepted
  - id: publish-by-digest
    decision: Publish CI-built images to a registry and have production pin them by digest.
    rationale: >-
      A host that builds from source runs whatever that host compiled that day; a digest pin makes
      the running artefact identical to the tested one and gives rollback without a rebuild. Local
      Dockerfiles still build without any registry, so contributors are unaffected.
    status: accepted
  - id: signing-key-holding-pattern
    decision: >-
      Deployment provisions the Ed25519 signing seed as a root-owned credential delivered to the
      control plane only; HSM or secure-element custody remains the Phase 1 target.
    rationale: >-
      The key-custody-hsm ADR requires HSM backing from Phase 1 and forbids a bare key file in
      production as the end state. Hardware is not yet built, so this flow makes the interim
      explicit, auditable, and never agent-adjacent instead of pretending the problem is solved.
    status: accepted
  - id: credentials-as-files
    decision: >-
      Every credential is resolved from a file when a *_FILE variable is set, falling back to the
      environment only otherwise; the production profile refuses the environment path outright for
      the signing key and the key-store token.
    rationale: >-
      Environment variables are readable from /proc/<pid>/environ, appear in container inspect
      output, and are inherited by every child process the sidecar spawns for injection. Since the
      deployment already has a file-based mechanism (systemd LoadCredential, Compose secrets),
      making files the preferred source costs nothing and removes a class of exposure that would
      otherwise be inherited by consumer processes. The production profile makes the preference
      enforceable rather than advisory, so the convention cannot be forgotten on a real host.
    status: accepted
implementation:
  primary: deploy/
  test_code: scripts/verify-hardening.sh
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - safekeys/sidecar
  - safekeys/control-plane
  - safekeys/envelope-encryption
  - safekeys/hardware-root
